WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:07.629
Arrows do bounce. You can
skip arrows off the ground.

00:00:07.629 --> 00:00:09.630
I've seen people skip arrows along water.

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:11.550
Hello! I'm Jim Kent,

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:13.620
known to the wider world as Grizzly Jim.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.770
I'm a traditional archer, YouTube creator.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.500
I'm lucky enough to have
traveled all over the world,

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.930
sharing my passion for
traditional archery.

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:23.340
Today I'm back to look at

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:26.250
some archery clips from film and TV

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:27.858
to judge how real they are.

00:00:30.275 --> 00:00:32.280
Mother: My boy!

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:33.240
He's at full draw there.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.670
He's got a pretty solid anchor.

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:38.850
You can see him massively
flinch as he shoots.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:40.730
Once you've been shooting for a while,

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:42.210
you get over that.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:43.470
You don't have that flinch.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.090
You don't anticipate the shot so much.

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:47.130
But when you're fairly new to archery,

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:49.163
that's something that's pretty typical.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:53.730
But he's given the feather a lick.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:55.800
I don't know why you'd do it.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:56.730
Why?

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:58.440
I mean, why would you lick it?

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.040
I mean, that's been in the dirt.

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:05.100
An arrow will spin as it
flies through the air,

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:06.600
especially with a feather.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:07.980
Feathers come in two flavors.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:09.600
You've got left wing or right wing.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.160
One will make it spin one way,

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:12.660
the other will make it spin the other.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:15.000
But it doesn't fly straight.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.040
Arrows, when they fly through the air,

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.960
they oscillate like crazy

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.700
because you've got all this energy

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.670
coming from the back and pushing.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:25.260
It's usually fairly weighty up front.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:26.220
It takes a couple of seconds

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:27.390
for the back to catch up with the front.

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:29.970
So you end up with this
hugely oscillating arrow

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:31.120
flying through the air.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.330
Obviously the more tension in the rope,

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:38.070
the easier it would be to cut.

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:39.210
If there wasn't much weight there

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:40.260
it would be very difficult to cut.

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:41.430
But if it's under a lot of tension,

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:43.355
that would cut through quite nicely.

00:01:49.357 --> 00:01:52.920
I don't know, to this day,
why he rips a feather off,

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.630
because they compress to almost nothing.

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:56.940
But you can shoot two arrows off a bow.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:57.900
You can do that.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:59.760
It's not an efficient way of doing things.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:00.780
The bow doesn't know

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:02.730
that there's two arrows on the bow,

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:04.950
the bow just thinks there's
one very heavy arrow.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:06.870
And obviously the heavier the arrow,

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.730
the more energy's used up

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:09.840
trying to propel that arrow.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:11.040
I think I'm going to have to go eight,

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:15.023
just because of the feather thing.

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:22.260
If I use a quiver, I tend
to use a back quiver.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:23.850
It gives you a nice low profile.

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:25.170
Quivers you tend to either wear

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:28.200
on your hip or your side or your back.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:29.220
When you're in the woods,

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:32.490
you tend to want to keep a
sort of fairly slim profile,

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:34.123
so a back quiver is quite useful.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:39.750
Arrows do make a noise
when you shoot them,

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:43.230
but they don't quite make a noise

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:44.970
like you expect them to,

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:47.490
unless you use something called a flu-flu,

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:50.100
which is an arrow with really
big feathers on the end.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.742
The noise that they make is so satisfying.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:59.100
Now, he's got this weird
grip on the string.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:03.360
He's got the arrow on the
left side of the bow, I think,

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:04.680
and he's holding the string like that,

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:06.570
which isn't what you do.

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:09.938
If you really wanted to
hold the string upside down,

00:03:09.938 --> 00:03:13.320
you'd need the arrow on
the right side of the bow.

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:17.528
I imagine that's more of an
aesthetic thing than anything.

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.460
You can hear, as he draws up,

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:25.350
you can hear the bow creaking.

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:26.520
Now, this is a trope.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:28.770
And that's when the bow
comes up to full draw,

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:31.213
you hear it creak. Bows don't creak.

00:03:31.213 --> 00:03:33.690
If your bow's creaking when
you come up to full draw,

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:35.580
it's probably going to explode on you.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:38.550
But the archery's a little
bit all over the shop.

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:42.180
I'm going to go six.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:44.490
Yeah. I'm going to get
some hate in the comments

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.465
for that one, but I love
"Lord of the Rings,"

00:03:46.465 --> 00:03:47.913
so don't come for me.

00:03:48.899 --> 00:03:50.034
Soldier: Archers!

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:54.990
I've tried this.

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:56.880
And what actually happens

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:00.990
is when you wrap something
around an arrow and shoot it,

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:03.270
the arrow goes, but that tends to stay.

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:04.350
So you end up with

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:07.950
a bit of linen on fire by your fingers,

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:09.240
which isn't a great idea.

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:10.860
I'm sure there was other ways of doing it,

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:14.610
but it was actually a small
cage on the end of the arrow

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:17.010
that had had something
flammable in the cage.

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:18.720
So when you shot the arrow,

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:20.130
the force of the arrow going forward

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:21.996
didn't force it down the arrow shaft.

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:28.620
It's all about quantity over quality.

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:32.130
So you'd send a lot of
arrows skyward, up and over,

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:34.140
to try and sort of rain down hell

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:35.973
on the opposing side.

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:41.640
There's a very famous English battle,

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:42.750
the battle of Agincourt,

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:46.080
where the English famously won the day

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:48.422
because of their archers
doing exactly that.

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:54.143
I kind of like the way the arrows

00:04:54.143 --> 00:04:56.918
are bouncing off the shields.
That's quite realistic.

00:05:00.492 --> 00:05:03.180
I would've definitely employed that tactic

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:04.922
if I was at Troy. Got a bracer on

00:05:04.922 --> 00:05:07.860
to protect his arm from the string.

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:09.202
So, what happens is,

00:05:09.202 --> 00:05:12.450
when you draw a bow back and let it go,

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:14.430
the string, in an ideal world,

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:17.460
would come nice and
straight past your arm,

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:19.752
but it doesn't. Because
it comes off the fingers,

00:05:19.752 --> 00:05:21.060
if you see a string in slow motion,

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:23.730
it actually comes towards
the bow like this.

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:25.710
So a lot of time you tend to

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:27.900
sort of bash your arm with the string,

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:29.160
and that sort of bracer there

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:32.490
would potentially stop that.

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:33.906
Give that one a seven.

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:42.240
She's got an anchor point
sort of under her chin.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:45.000
She's looking very, very straight,

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.640
very good sort of target-archery style.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:50.220
And there's nothing wrong
with target-archery style,

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:53.310
but it is very good for
shooting set distances.

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:55.590
She's going to be competing
in the Hunger Games,

00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:57.330
so she's going to be in sort of

00:05:57.330 --> 00:05:58.920
more combat sort of situations,

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:02.730
so that isn't really the
right sort of archery

00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:04.589
for that sort of scenario.

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:11.010
She's going very quickly,

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:12.630
and you see it a lot in films

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:14.730
where people are shooting very quickly.

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:16.860
Archery, for me anyway,

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:20.070
is always a little bit more measured,

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:23.130
a little bit more "take
your time" a little bit,

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:26.505
like get that perfect shot off

00:06:26.505 --> 00:06:27.810
because it takes a long time

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:29.850
to kind of load your bow
up again and shoot again.

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:32.500
In the Middle East, they
do sort of archery where

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:34.260
he's very quickly loading the bow,

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:37.201
shooting very rapidly,
and it's an amazing skill.

00:06:37.201 --> 00:06:39.780
I imagine the bow is a very low poundage,

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:43.110
because the poundage required
to actually do any damage,

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:45.360
you're going to need sort of
a fair bit of poundage there,

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:46.710
and it takes time to pull it back.

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:49.123
So she's not really shooting with

00:06:49.123 --> 00:06:52.131
the greatest amount of kind
of back tension and stuff.

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:59.370
At that short distance, that would work.

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:01.140
It's not the prettiest, but, you know,

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:02.190
in that sort of situation,

00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:03.330
you're not trying to be elegant,

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:04.163
you're not trying to be pretty,

00:07:04.163 --> 00:07:05.730
you're trying to save your life.

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:07.590
Visually, very impressive.

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:10.680
But you wouldn't use that
technique in a competition.

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:12.120
I'll give that one an eight.

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:14.790
Some of the movements are
a little bit out there.

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:18.015
It's not necessarily right,
but it's quite realistic.

00:07:22.795 --> 00:07:24.720
175 yards away.

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:26.730
He's talking about a very long way

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:30.030
to be very accurate with a longbow.

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:34.320
Now, I'm sure people were
pretty accurate back then,

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:37.260
but the equipment was very basic,

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:39.210
the arrows were very basic.

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:41.370
Archery's all about consistency.

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:44.280
And in these sort of times,

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:45.840
you wouldn't have had the technology

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:48.210
to make an arrow exactly
the same as another arrow.

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:49.680
You couldn't weigh them exactly the same.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:50.888
They can't see us.

00:07:52.210 --> 00:07:53.630
Maybe they thought that was luck.

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:58.800
They were typically sort
of about as tall as you.

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:00.480
So you know, 6 foot plus.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:03.330
For really smacking an arrow in,

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:06.810
a longbow really does
hammer the arrows home.

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:09.900
Very big draw force curve, lot of energy.

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:12.660
If you look at sort of more war bows,

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:16.140
which is a very high-poundage
English longbow,

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:18.450
sort of 80 pounds plus.

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:23.070
The bow I shoot is about 45
to 50 pounds in draw weight,

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:25.170
which is quite substantial.

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:27.210
I shot a 120-pound bow once,

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:29.552
and it damn near folded me in half.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:35.100
He's put his hand in his pouch,

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:37.020
and he's putting a powder on his fingers,

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:38.580
possibly a resin or something.

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:39.630
I'm guessing it's to help him

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:42.180
get a smooth release on the string.

00:08:46.552 --> 00:08:48.480
There's not a lot wrong with that.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:49.860
It looks pretty solid.

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:51.720
His head barely moves.

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:53.760
A lot of people, when they're
first getting into archery,

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:56.160
will be so focused on
where the arrow's going,

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:58.230
they'll shoot and they'll move their head

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:00.455
to try and look past the
bow and see where it is.

00:09:00.455 --> 00:09:02.670
He makes the shot, his head doesn't move,

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:03.503
and I like that.

00:09:03.503 --> 00:09:06.632
That's a really good technique.

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:10.530
You missed him anyway.

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:11.680
I didn't miss anyone.

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:15.492
If you can see through it,
you can shoot through it.

00:09:15.492 --> 00:09:17.520
You don't need a huge amount of space.

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:19.800
Obviously the smaller the space,

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:22.117
the more chance you've got
of something going wrong.

00:09:22.117 --> 00:09:24.278
The arrows don't fly straight,

00:09:24.278 --> 00:09:27.300
so if you're shooting
through something too small,

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:28.357
as the arrow flies through,

00:09:28.357 --> 00:09:29.550
you could clip the back of it.

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:30.978
In hunting situations,

00:09:30.978 --> 00:09:34.110
arrows quite often pass through an animal.

00:09:34.110 --> 00:09:35.040
You call it pass-through.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:37.200
But at that sort of distance,

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:39.420
the arrow's going to lose a lot of energy.

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:41.430
Because what feathers do,

00:09:41.430 --> 00:09:44.640
feathers stabilize an arrow fantastically,

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:46.080
but they also act as a parachute,

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:47.760
so they're going to slow the arrow down,

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:49.620
and there were some quite
big feathers on that arrow.

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:51.870
The bigger the feathers, the more stable,

00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:53.400
but the slower it's going to go.

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:55.770
So, you know, 175 yards,

00:09:55.770 --> 00:09:57.540
the arrow's going to slow down a bit.

00:09:57.540 --> 00:09:59.348
I don't know if you'd get a pass-through

00:09:59.348 --> 00:10:02.130
at that sort of distance.

00:10:02.130 --> 00:10:04.564
So we'll go seven. Sorry, King Arthur.

00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:13.365
These guys have shot three arrows

00:10:13.365 --> 00:10:15.848
into a cement wall.

00:10:15.848 --> 00:10:19.110
If you shoot an arrow into something hard,

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:22.680
it bounces out, especially if it's stone.

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:24.540
Any archer that's been
shooting for a while

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:26.880
and has taken a bad
miss and has hit a stone

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:29.100
or missed the target and hit a wall,

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:31.530
it absolutely pancakes the arrow.

00:10:31.530 --> 00:10:34.350
I can't actually see the types of points

00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:35.550
they're using on these arrows,

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:37.380
but if they're trying to kill people,

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:39.750
which it looks like they
are in a "John Wick" movie,

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:42.300
they'd be using something
called a broadhead.

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:44.700
But a broadheaded arrow is an arrow tip

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:48.030
that's designed to cause
as much damage as possible.

00:10:48.030 --> 00:10:50.610
It's usually a two-bladed, three-bladed

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:52.210
sharp point on the end of an arrow.

00:10:56.010 --> 00:11:00.090
They're doing some very
close-range combat.

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:03.150
There is sort of some evidence of kind of

00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:04.740
the more martial side of archery.

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:08.490
Generally, close-range archery is,

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:10.065
I don't know, it's not really a thing.

00:11:10.065 --> 00:11:14.130
Archery's a long-range, mid-
to long-range discipline.

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:17.550
It takes seconds to load the bow,

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:19.620
to draw the bow, to shoot the bow.

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:23.223
A guy with a sword could
do the job a lot quicker.

00:11:26.010 --> 00:11:28.740
They're definitely the more
sort of Eastern-style bow,

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:30.810
which is really a precursor

00:11:30.810 --> 00:11:32.970
to the modern-day recurve bow.

00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:36.461
She's using it as a
club, which you could do,

00:11:36.461 --> 00:11:39.450
but they're generally fairly
light, these sorts of bows,

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:41.220
and she's kicking the s---

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:43.782
out of guys with armor with a bow.

00:11:43.782 --> 00:11:47.673
I don't know how effective that would be,

00:11:48.590 --> 00:11:50.760
you know, to guys in armor.

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:53.220
They're not very heavy
pieces of equipment.

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:55.800
They're made of mostly, sort of,

00:11:55.800 --> 00:11:57.600
a wood or a wood substitute.

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:00.780
We'll go seven, just because
it's such a good scene.

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:02.190
It's really dynamic.

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:04.398
It shows different styles of archery.

00:12:09.930 --> 00:12:11.280
That would be very heavy,

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:13.500
and that would create a
lot of weight in the arrow,

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:16.890
so the arrow would probably

00:12:16.890 --> 00:12:17.760
just flop in front of her.

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:20.940
And also, she's got to
shoot it over her hand.

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:23.340
But the thing I really like
about that little section there

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:25.260
is the way the bow moves.

00:12:25.260 --> 00:12:29.797
Now, if you do happen to
film a bow in slow motion,

00:12:29.797 --> 00:12:33.090
they are like a wet noodle.

00:12:33.090 --> 00:12:34.920
They're wobbling all over the place.

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:36.540
It's fascinating to see

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:38.580
when you see something
like that in slow motion.

00:12:38.580 --> 00:12:41.520
And they've got the movement
of the bow completely right.

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:43.453
That is exactly what a bow would do.

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:48.750
There are people out there

00:12:48.750 --> 00:12:51.810
that have claimed to catch arrows.

00:12:51.810 --> 00:12:53.550
When I have seen it done,

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:55.860
it's kind of more stunts where

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:58.800
the arrow isn't traveling
particularly hard,

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:00.990
isn't traveling particularly fast,

00:13:00.990 --> 00:13:03.492
and you sort of grab it out of the air.

00:13:03.492 --> 00:13:04.860
I've seen it done like that.

00:13:04.860 --> 00:13:08.430
But in a true situation with a true bow

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:10.975
at a realistic poundage,
I think you'd struggle,

00:13:10.975 --> 00:13:12.570
unless it was at a very long distance

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:14.790
and you could sort of
track it and whatnot.

00:13:14.790 --> 00:13:15.840
I mean, don't do it,

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:18.450
because you're essentially
shooting an arrow at someone,

00:13:18.450 --> 00:13:21.660
which is stupid, so don't ever do that.

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:22.710
I mean, they're dealing with

00:13:22.710 --> 00:13:24.750
some kind of fantasy elements there,

00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:26.467
but we'll give that one an eight, I think.

00:13:33.613 --> 00:13:34.740
I love this film.

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:37.890
This is such a good archery movie.

00:13:37.890 --> 00:13:39.510
This guy here at full draw,

00:13:39.510 --> 00:13:41.580
now, the bow he's using is

00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:44.970
a nice little short-style hunting bow.

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:47.580
He's got really, really nice form there.

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:48.900
He's wearing finger protection,

00:13:48.900 --> 00:13:50.640
and that's the first time we've seen

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:53.340
someone actually wear
proper finger protection

00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:56.015
in any of these scenes.
And if you shoot a bow

00:13:56.015 --> 00:13:57.810
without any sort of finger protection,

00:13:57.810 --> 00:14:00.400
be that a glove, be that a tab,

00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:02.280
it's going to hurt,

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:03.630
and over time it's going to cause

00:14:03.630 --> 00:14:05.790
serious damage to your fingers.

00:14:05.790 --> 00:14:07.740
First with blisters, then nerve damage.

00:14:11.093 --> 00:14:11.926
Release.

00:14:11.926 --> 00:14:15.300
Some people really do
prefer to close one eye

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:18.720
and use their dominant
eye to look at the target.

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:20.700
They're probably using some sort of system

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:23.647
where they're using their eye
and the point of the arrow

00:14:23.647 --> 00:14:25.650
and sort of working out the distance

00:14:25.650 --> 00:14:28.560
and where they need to put
that arrow on the target

00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:29.970
to make the shot.

00:14:29.970 --> 00:14:33.000
I don't do that. I keep both eyes open.

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:34.350
We see in 3D,

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:37.380
so I like to see what it
is I'm shooting at in 3D.

00:14:37.380 --> 00:14:41.580
I get a bit more of a better
perspective for where it is,

00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:42.810
how far away it is.

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:45.252
It's really good for distance judging.

00:14:49.020 --> 00:14:51.660
Now, this is why I like
this scene so much.

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:55.560
He's going through something
now called target panic.

00:14:55.560 --> 00:14:57.510
And that is a mental issue.

00:14:57.510 --> 00:15:01.110
I mean, it's a very extreme
version of target panic,

00:15:01.110 --> 00:15:03.090
but he's going through something,

00:15:03.090 --> 00:15:06.150
and you get it with hunting quite a lot,

00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:08.190
you get it with sort of competitions.

00:15:08.190 --> 00:15:09.540
You just get into your own head,

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:11.880
and it's very difficult to get a shot off.

00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:15.450
And it's so good to see a representation

00:15:15.450 --> 00:15:18.630
of someone really
struggling with the shot.

00:15:18.630 --> 00:15:20.250
And I've struggled with target panic.

00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:23.010
In films, you see everyone's
shooting, going crazy,

00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:25.020
doing everything perfectly every time.

00:15:25.020 --> 00:15:27.245
But archery isn't perfect every time.

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:36.090
And what they're doing
is they're bowfishing.

00:15:36.090 --> 00:15:39.060
He's got a bow quiver
attached to the side of it,

00:15:39.060 --> 00:15:40.980
and he's got a fishing reel.

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:42.510
You can tell he's been coached

00:15:42.510 --> 00:15:44.850
by someone that knows what they're doing.

00:15:44.850 --> 00:15:47.220
Bowfishing is an incredibly tough skill,

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:48.630
and I've got nothing but respect

00:15:48.630 --> 00:15:49.800
for anyone that can bowfish.

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:52.530
Because it's not just a
case of seeing the fish.

00:15:52.530 --> 00:15:53.970
Because of the refraction of the water,

00:15:53.970 --> 00:15:55.230
you've actually got to put it

00:15:55.230 --> 00:15:57.237
in a different place to where the fish is.

00:15:57.237 --> 00:16:02.220
It's a whole skill that I've
got nothing but respect for.

00:16:02.220 --> 00:16:04.500
Machines are going to fail,

00:16:04.500 --> 00:16:06.240
and the system's going to fail.

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:09.881
He's got some very bright feathers

00:16:09.881 --> 00:16:11.522
on his arrow.

00:16:11.522 --> 00:16:12.630
Now, they're what's called,

00:16:12.630 --> 00:16:14.850
the two shapes you tend to get,

00:16:14.850 --> 00:16:17.190
you get a shield shape
and a parabolic shape.

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:18.570
They're a parabolic feather,

00:16:18.570 --> 00:16:19.890
so they're round at the back.

00:16:19.890 --> 00:16:21.690
Very bright colors.

00:16:21.690 --> 00:16:25.410
Hunters tend to use quite
bright colors on their arrows.

00:16:25.410 --> 00:16:27.930
Sort of the oranges,
the pinks, the whites.

00:16:27.930 --> 00:16:31.270
They show up in a nature
environment quite well.

00:16:31.270 --> 00:16:32.499
They help you track the animal,

00:16:32.499 --> 00:16:34.890
if you're looking for the arrow.

00:16:34.890 --> 00:16:36.810
They help you find lost arrows.

00:16:36.810 --> 00:16:38.940
I think it's a very
realistic representation,

00:16:38.940 --> 00:16:41.390
so I am going to give
"Deliverance" a nine.

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:47.576
Bows don't do that.

00:16:47.576 --> 00:16:51.150
Bows are under a lot of tension.

00:16:51.150 --> 00:16:54.930
I can't think of a plausible
way that would ever work.

00:16:54.930 --> 00:16:58.230
You'd have to string the bow.

00:16:58.230 --> 00:17:00.210
You'd have to put tension into the bow.

00:17:00.210 --> 00:17:01.470
That's not how bows work.

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:04.290
It looks very cool. It's
very visually stunning.

00:17:04.290 --> 00:17:05.370
To shoot something far away,

00:17:05.370 --> 00:17:06.396
you have to shoot above it.

00:17:06.396 --> 00:17:10.293
The closer something gets to your level,

00:17:10.293 --> 00:17:12.143
the lower you have to get.

00:17:16.020 --> 00:17:18.660
Same with if you were
clay-pigeon shooting.

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:20.340
You kind of get a lead on the arrow,

00:17:20.340 --> 00:17:23.340
so you're not shooting where the thing is

00:17:23.340 --> 00:17:25.140
that you're shooting at is,

00:17:25.140 --> 00:17:26.910
you're shooting it where it's going to be.

00:17:26.910 --> 00:17:29.357
An arrow does travel.

00:17:29.357 --> 00:17:32.220
It travels fast, but
not as fast as a bullet.

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:35.641
Tracking it requires more
of an instinctive style.

00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:42.990
Theoretically could put
ballistics in an arrow.

00:17:42.990 --> 00:17:44.370
You can certainly get arrows

00:17:44.370 --> 00:17:47.130
that have got gunpowder in it and a charge

00:17:47.130 --> 00:17:50.100
that sort of causes a lot of damage.

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:52.140
It's really hard to shoot in a helmet,

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:54.000
but he's got a very,

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:55.560
he's overdrawing the bow as well.

00:17:55.560 --> 00:17:58.172
He's pulling it back right back to here,

00:17:58.172 --> 00:18:01.440
which is a technique that can be used.

00:18:01.440 --> 00:18:03.510
I was in a little film once

00:18:03.510 --> 00:18:06.120
where I had to try and
shoot in a gas mask,

00:18:06.120 --> 00:18:09.690
and you can't get an
anchor point in a gas mask.

00:18:09.690 --> 00:18:10.590
It's really difficult.

00:18:10.590 --> 00:18:14.340
Arrows do bounce. You can
skip arrows off the ground.

00:18:14.340 --> 00:18:16.350
I've seen people skip arrows along water.

00:18:16.350 --> 00:18:18.060
There's a trick I used to do,

00:18:18.060 --> 00:18:19.683
and there's a little wooden ramp,

00:18:19.683 --> 00:18:22.080
and I could shoot the
arrow at the wooden ramp

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:23.070
and it would skip up.

00:18:23.070 --> 00:18:24.003
I'm going four.

00:18:26.610 --> 00:18:27.443
Pull.

00:18:31.050 --> 00:18:34.300
What he's got there, he's
taken, what, three, four arrows?

00:18:34.300 --> 00:18:37.800
He's going to hold them
in his hand as he shoots.

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:39.150
So he's not using a quiver,

00:18:39.150 --> 00:18:41.546
he's using his own hand as the quiver.

00:18:41.546 --> 00:18:44.040
And it's a technique where you sort of

00:18:44.040 --> 00:18:45.870
draw the arrow from your hand

00:18:45.870 --> 00:18:47.640
almost onto the bow straightaway.

00:18:47.640 --> 00:18:48.473
As I mentioned before,

00:18:48.473 --> 00:18:50.907
the kind of more Eastern
style of shooting,

00:18:50.907 --> 00:18:53.622
very quick succession.

00:18:53.622 --> 00:18:55.998
Again, getting as many arrows
in the air as possible.

00:19:02.700 --> 00:19:04.893
He's got this very low anchor point,

00:19:05.820 --> 00:19:07.320
very floating anchor point.

00:19:07.320 --> 00:19:10.440
That's difficult to get accuracy,

00:19:10.440 --> 00:19:14.108
but he's using an Eastern-style bow,

00:19:14.108 --> 00:19:16.980
the sort of bow that
you'd use on a horseback,

00:19:16.980 --> 00:19:19.260
and you can't really get
a good solid anchor point

00:19:19.260 --> 00:19:20.130
when you're on a horseback.

00:19:20.130 --> 00:19:22.830
And some of those archers
are phenomenally accurate.

00:19:22.830 --> 00:19:25.380
So maybe anchor point isn't everything

00:19:25.380 --> 00:19:26.607
for this sort of particular style.

00:19:26.607 --> 00:19:29.130
And I love Pedro Pascal.
I love Matt Damon.

00:19:29.130 --> 00:19:32.180
Ah, I'm going to give it a five.

00:19:37.050 --> 00:19:40.200
He's got a crossbow with a scope on it.

00:19:40.200 --> 00:19:42.150
Now, the thing is, with a crossbow,

00:19:42.150 --> 00:19:43.443
they are very accurate.

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:47.670
It takes a lot of the
human error out of the bow,

00:19:47.670 --> 00:19:49.620
so it's literally pulling a trigger,

00:19:49.620 --> 00:19:51.091
as long as it's cocked correctly

00:19:51.091 --> 00:19:53.058
and you've got the bolt in.

00:19:58.103 --> 00:20:02.100
I did notice, he shoots the bolt,

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:05.130
but the the bow still remains cocked.

00:20:05.130 --> 00:20:07.410
She's holding it for a very long time.

00:20:07.410 --> 00:20:10.320
That's a very long time
to hold a trad bow.

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:12.360
Olympic archery is slightly different.

00:20:12.360 --> 00:20:16.050
You tend to have a
little device on your bow

00:20:16.050 --> 00:20:17.760
that sort of clicks when the arrow

00:20:17.760 --> 00:20:18.930
gets to a certain length,

00:20:18.930 --> 00:20:21.600
and you hold it full draw for
a bit and expand on the shot.

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:24.122
So the shot sequence on

00:20:24.122 --> 00:20:27.870
an Olympic-style bow would be longer,

00:20:27.870 --> 00:20:31.590
but she's at full draw
for a very long time.

00:20:31.590 --> 00:20:34.200
The other thing I noticed
about her being at full draw,

00:20:34.200 --> 00:20:36.510
the way she's got the string,

00:20:36.510 --> 00:20:38.643
it's down the side of her nose.

00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:40.920
Now, what's going to happen,

00:20:40.920 --> 00:20:44.580
if that was a fully
fledged, full-poundage bow

00:20:44.580 --> 00:20:46.170
and she had a decent release,

00:20:46.170 --> 00:20:48.270
that's going to whip right past her nose

00:20:48.270 --> 00:20:51.450
and cause a lot of pain.
It's going to hurt.

00:20:51.450 --> 00:20:55.291
And if you do it again and
again and again, it's horrible.

00:20:59.688 --> 00:21:03.390
I imagine there is some sort
of indexing on the arrow

00:21:03.390 --> 00:21:04.680
for her to sort of feel.

00:21:04.680 --> 00:21:06.390
On modern arrows, you tend to have

00:21:06.390 --> 00:21:09.780
a little plastic index notch on there,

00:21:09.780 --> 00:21:13.380
so you can feel which
orientation the arrow is.

00:21:13.380 --> 00:21:16.140
So I imagine it's a
similar sort of situation.

00:21:16.140 --> 00:21:17.850
Let's give "The Hunger Games" a six.

00:21:17.850 --> 00:21:20.340
My favorite kind of archery movies,

00:21:20.340 --> 00:21:21.360
I mean, two of them are in there.

00:21:21.360 --> 00:21:22.650
The "Robin Hood: Prince of Thieves,"

00:21:22.650 --> 00:21:24.580
which I think is absolutely fantastic.

00:21:24.580 --> 00:21:25.830
It's a little bit cheesy.

00:21:25.830 --> 00:21:27.330
The other one in there is "Deliverance."

00:21:27.330 --> 00:21:29.430
It's such a good
representation of archery.

00:21:29.430 --> 00:21:31.996
It shows archery as, it's never perfect.

00:21:31.996 --> 00:21:34.080
There's always something
that can go wrong.

00:21:34.080 --> 00:21:35.310
And if you like this video,

00:21:35.310 --> 00:21:38.560
why not aim at the one above
my head and check it out?

